━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

1. DEFINITIONS

   "Licensor" means Adam Camerer, copyright holder of the Software.

   "Software" means the Meridian source code, binaries, documentation,
   and all associated files distributed under this license.

   "Protection Period" means six (6) years from the date each version
   of the Software is first publicly distributed. Each version carries
   its own independent Protection Period from its own release date.

   "Change Date" means the date six (6) years after the first public
   distribution date of a given version of the Software.

   "Change License" means the MIT License
   (https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT).

   "Local Use" means installation and operation of the Software on
   machines owned or controlled by you, where each user operates their
   own running instance. Remote access to a centrally hosted instance
   does not qualify as Local Use.

   "Hosted Database" means an external database service (such as
   PostgreSQL on Neon, Railway, Supabase, or similar providers) used
   as the data backend for locally-running instances. Connecting to a
   Hosted Database does not by itself constitute hosting the Software.

   "Production Use" means making the Software available as a service
   to external third parties — i.e., offering Meridian or a Competing
   Product as a hosted or managed service that others connect to.
   Internal use of the Software within your own organization, including
   use that generates revenue for your own business, is not Production
   Use and is explicitly permitted under Section 2.

   "Competing Product" means any software, service, or platform that:
   a) provides shared goal state, task queue, and session coordination
      functionality to multiple human users or AI agents, AND
   b) is made available to third parties as a hosted or managed service.

   Mere similarity in features does not create a Competing Product.
   The conjunction of (a) and (b) is required.

   "Prohibited Publishing" means distributing materials — including but
   not limited to Docker images, Helm charts, or hosted deployment
   guides — whose primary purpose is enabling third parties to operate
   the Software as a commercial or multi-tenant service. Writing blog
   posts, documentation, tutorials, or internal deployment scripts
   for personal or internal use does not constitute Prohibited Publishing.

2. ADDITIONAL USE GRANT (FREE USES)

   Notwithstanding the Production Use restriction in Section 3, the
   following uses are explicitly permitted free of charge during the
   Protection Period:

   a) Local Use for any purpose — personal, commercial, or internal —
      regardless of team or organization size. There is no seat limit.

   b) Connecting locally-running instances to a Hosted Database for
      team coordination, regardless of team size.

   c) Deploying the Software on infrastructure owned or controlled by
      your organization for internal use only, provided no Prohibited
      Publishing occurs.

   d) Running the Software in local or self-owned CI/CD pipelines and
      automated workflows for your own projects.

   e) Using the Software for academic, research, and educational
      purposes, including publication of research findings.

   f) Distributing unmodified copies for evaluation or non-commercial
      purposes, provided this license is retained in full.

3. PRODUCTION USE RESTRICTION

   During the Protection Period, Production Use of the Software is
   not permitted without a separate written commercial license from
   the Licensor, specifically including:

   a) Offering the Software or a Competing Product as a hosted or
      managed service to third parties, whether for a fee or free.

   b) Prohibited Publishing as defined in Section 1.

   c) Selling, sublicensing, or distributing the Software or derivatives
      as part of any commercial product or service for third parties.

   d) Removing, altering, or obscuring this license or any copyright
      notices included with the Software.

4. LICENSE CONVERSION (CHANGE LICENSE)

   On each version's Change Date, that version of the Software will
   automatically be made available under the Change License (MIT).
   This conversion is irrevocable and requires no further action.
   Subsequent versions may carry their own Protection Period and
   Change Date under a new MSL notice.

   The MIT License grants you perpetual, worldwide, royalty-free
   permission to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute,
   sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Software without restriction,
   subject only to the copyright notice and license text being retained.

5. ATTRIBUTION

   Any permitted use, modification, or redistribution must retain this
   license text, the copyright notice, and original attribution intact.

6. CONTRIBUTIONS

   By submitting any contribution to this project, the contributor
